# QuantaHub Master Governance

This policy is mandatory on every Agent task. Do not decide whether to load another QuantaHub rule: all QuantaHub rules are Always Apply.

## Execution authority order

When instructions conflict, use this order:

1. Explicit current user requirement.
2. Non-negotiable security/trust-boundary invariants.
3. Accepted decisions in docs/DECISIONS.md.
4. QuantaHub rules and canonical docs.
5. Existing project conventions and pinned dependencies.
6. Deterministic fallback in docs/DECISION_POLICY.md.

A user-requested architecture change must update the decision log and affected rules/docs in the same change. Never silently weaken a security invariant.

## Before every meaningful implementation

1. Read the task against all active QuantaHub rules.
2. Inspect existing code/config before creating a parallel implementation.
3. Identify affected boundaries: Platform/CTFd, Lab Engine, Security/Infra, Frontend, Data, Supply Chain.
4. Use the required specialist agents automatically.
5. Implement the smallest complete solution consistent with policy.
6. Run required verification.
7. Update canonical documentation when a durable decision or contract changes.

## No unnecessary questions

Do not ask the user to choose between equivalent engineering approaches when policy can decide. Apply the deterministic fallback and continue.

Ask only when a missing decision is genuinely product/business/legal in nature, or when two requirements are irreconcilable without changing user intent.

## Hard invariants

- Prefer CTFd extension points over deep core forks.
- CTFd never owns Docker/containerd/hypervisor privileges.
- Control plane and intentionally vulnerable lab plane remain separate trust domains.
- Labs are hostile and multi-tenant isolation is mandatory.
- Default lab egress is deny.
- Production lab browser origins are separate from platform/auth origins.
- Open-source lab artifacts are pinned, reviewed and published through the controlled intake pipeline.
- Durable state uses PostgreSQL as source of truth unless an accepted decision changes this.
- Meaningful implementation is not complete without verifier evidence.
- Security-boundary changes are not complete without security review.

Canonical architecture is policy, not a suggestion.
